WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.949
Challenging weather can turn a routine mission into an unsafe or low-value operation.

00:00:06.949 --> 00:00:17.733
Wind, gusts, turbulence, moisture, fog, heat, cold, smoke, dust, visibility, and rapid change affect the aircraft, crew, sensors, battery, and evidence.

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:28.995
Plan with current approved information, forecast timing, surface and altitude effects, local terrain, precipitation, visibility, temperature, aircraft guidance, and mission data requirements.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:34.507
Define go or no-go criteria before pressure to fly develops.

00:00:34.507 --> 00:00:43.853
At the site, compare forecast with reality, inspect launch and recovery, brief the crew, and verify emergency options.

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:51.760
During flight, monitor aircraft behavior, groundspeed, power use, battery, signal, visibility, moisture, and image quality.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.272
Review return-to-home assumptions and preserve manual and emergency landing options.

00:00:57.272 --> 00:01:01.825
Abort early when conditions or data quality deteriorate.

00:01:01.825 --> 00:01:06.857
Protect people and property, then document anomalies and lessons.

00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:17.641
The goal is safe, lawful, useful flight data, not forcing a mission through conditions that exceed aircraft, crew, or mission limits.

00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:23.631
Delaying or canceling is successful risk management when margin is inadequate.
